userStories.description:
**Goal** — Make Memory rows readable and name their Kind and Status filters without changing stored values or backend behavior.

**Motivation** — R2-2: the Memory page shows subjects as raw `ticket:<cuid>` / `agent:<cuid>`, ticket-status objects as raw enums (`IN_PROGRESS`), and kinds as raw codes (`INCIDENT_PATTERN`).

**Approach** — Add `memorySubject` in `apps/web/lib/memory-subject.ts`. In `pages/[project]/memory.vue`, render a ticket subject as the label `Ticket` plus the short id, linked to `/<slug>/tickets/<id>`; an agent subject as the label `Agent` plus the short id; any other subject unchanged; the raw subject as the `title`. Render a `status` object through `tickets.status.*` when that key exists, and kinds through new `memory.kinds.*` keys (falling back to `humanizeCode`), in the table and in the Kind filter options. Associate the Kind and Status filter labels with their selects. Add `memory.kinds.*` (FACT, DECISION, PREFERENCE, CONSTRAINT, INCIDENT_PATTERN, NOTE) and `memory.subject.ticket` / `memory.subject.agent` to `en.json` and `zh.json`.

No API change. Memory subjects are not resolved to ticket refs (that needs an API lookup); a ticket subject links to `/<slug>/tickets/<cuid>`, which the ticket route already accepts (`GET /projects/:slug/tickets/:ref` takes "KODA-42 or CUID").

A shortened id is the first 8 characters of the id. The full raw value stays available as the element's `title`.

Accessible names come from a `<label for>` / control `id` pair where a visible label already sits above the control, and from `aria-label` where the control has no visible label of its own (the per-row notification checkboxes).

Readable labels reuse existing helpers: `codeLabel(t, te, prefix, code)` from `~/lib/fleet-i18n` (translation or the raw code), `humanizeCode(code)` from `~/lib/humanize-code` (words for a code with no key), the `tickets.status.*` keys, the `home.activity.eventType.*` keys and `fleet.command.*` / `fleet.state.*`. For this story, missing kind keys use humanizeCode; a missing ticket status key preserves the original object.

**Scope** — In: Memory display, the two filter associations, the pure subject parser, both web locales, and Jest coverage. Out: Timeline labels/member lookup (US-002), flow labels (US-003), ingest project labels (US-004), permission/loading UI (US-005), notification/SLO controls (US-006), and board layout (US-007). Resolving memory `ticket:<cuid>` subjects to ticket refs (`SB-10`); it needs an API lookup.

**Interface** — New module, verbatim contract:
```ts
// apps/web/lib/memory-subject.ts
export interface MemorySubject {
  type: 'ticket' | 'agent' | 'other'
  /** The part after the `ticket:` / `agent:` prefix; the whole subject for `other`. */
  id: string
  /** `id` cut to its first 8 characters. */
  short: string
}
// `ticket:` / `agent:` with nothing after the prefix is `other` (the whole subject is the id).
// Only the first colon splits: `ticket:a:b` has id `a:b`.
export function memorySubject(subject: string): MemorySubject
```

**Tests and regression anchors** — Create tests/lib/memory-subject.spec.ts and tests/pages/memory-readable.spec.ts. Page and component ACs mount with `mountSfc` (`apps/web/tests/helpers/mount-sfc.ts`) and assert on the rendered DOM, as `tests/pages/notification-settings-page.spec.ts` does. Add the new subject, kind and status template bindings to createRenderContext in tests/pages/memory.spec.ts; keep the `{{ ... item.subject ... }}` interpolation pins and every other existing assertion as they are. Add `te: () => false` to the useI18n stubs in memory-mount.spec.ts and memory-retire-visibility.spec.ts and to the page-level i18nStub() in memory-retire-flow.spec.ts if absent; every existing assertion stays as it is. Preserve NOTE expansion, filtering payloads, pagination, scope badges and retirement permissions/flows.
